        <div id="fence-vs-deck" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why a Redwood Fence Is Not Just a Vertical Deck
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every rule of thumb you read about deck stain was written for a horizontal surface that holds standing water, takes direct overhead sun for most of the day and gets ground down by shoes, chair legs and dog claws. A fence board gets none of that foot traffic, sheds rain almost as fast as it lands and only takes full sun on one face at a time. That changes how long a finish lasts, which in turn changes which finish makes sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            The makers say so themselves. Ready Seal publishes a life expectancy of one to three years on horizontal surfaces and three to five years on vertical surfaces, and recommends a light clean and recoat every two years on a deck but every three to four years on a fence or siding. The dealer that runs the TWP help site makes the same point from the other side: when it explains its wet-on-wet method it notes that verticals generally last about twice as long as the floor. Treat those as the makers' ratings, not guarantees, but the direction is consistent across every maker's guidance we read.
          </p>

          <p>
            What a fence does have is weak points a deck mostly hides. The cut tops of pickets and the tops of posts are end grain, which drinks water like a bundle of straws and is where checking and rot start. The bottom inch of every board sits in the splash zone, often in contact with mulch or soil. Rails trap water on their top edge. And the back side, which you probably cannot see from the house, faces your neighbour and gets exactly the same weather as the side you care about.
          </p>

          <p>
            Redwood itself helps. The heartwood, the red-brown part of the tree, carries the extractives that give it natural decay and insect resistance, and the California Redwood Association recommends heartwood grades such as Construction Heart or Deck Heart for posts and anything on or near the ground. Sapwood-containing grades like Construction Common, which show cream-coloured streaks, are rated for above-ground uses such as fence boards and rails. Knowing which grade you have tells you how much the finish is protecting appearance and how much it is protecting the wood.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the question for a redwood fence is less about which can is strongest and more about which finish matches how much colour you want to keep, how often you are willing to recoat, and whether you can reach both sides. The rest of this guide answers those in order, and our <a href="/best-redwood-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">redwood sealer rankings</a> cover the individual products in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="finish-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Clear, Semi-Transparent or Solid: Choosing the Opacity
          </h2>

          <p>
            Opacity is the decision that matters most, because it sets both the look and the recoat interval. The California Redwood Association's exterior finishes guide groups the options into clear water repellents, bleaching or weathering stains, semi-transparent stains, solid-body stains and paint, and its maintenance guidance for each is a useful baseline because it is not trying to sell you a can.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clear water repellents and clear sealers</h3>

          <p>
            A clear sealer with a mildewcide keeps water out and, in the CRA's words, stabilises redwood at a buckskin tan, but it does not stop sunlight from breaking down the surface. The CRA notes that areas exposed to direct sun and rain may eventually bleach to grey, and that in humid or harsher climates reapplication may be needed every 12 to 18 months. Ready Seal's own FAQ makes the same point more bluntly: clear finishes let UV pass straight through to the wood. If someone is looking for the best clear sealer for a redwood fence, the honest answer is that clear buys water protection and a short-lived natural colour, and it means a recoat roughly every year or so on any sunny face.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Semi-transparent stains</h3>

          <p>
            This is where most redwood fences belong. A semi-transparent penetrating stain carries enough pigment to block a meaningful share of UV while letting the grain and the natural variation of the boards show through. The CRA recommends oil-based products in this category and gives a refinishing interval of three to five years, with one refinish coat usually enough. A redwood-toned semi-transparent also evens out the colour difference between heartwood and sapwood boards, which on a Construction Common fence is often the main reason people stain at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solid-colour stains and paint</h3>

          <p>
            Solid stain hides the grain but keeps the texture, and the CRA again recommends oil-based versions with a three-to-five-year refinish cycle. Paint gets the longest interval in the CRA chart, seven to ten years, but it needs a stain-blocking primer, it is the hardest finish to remove later, and once it starts to peel a fence becomes a scraping job. Film-forming finishes are also less forgiving of redwood's water-soluble extractives, which we cover below. Our <a href="/solid-vs-semi-transparent-deck-stain/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">solid vs semi-transparent comparison</a> goes deeper into the trade-off.
          </p>

